# 2. THE TWO REQUIRED PRIMARY OPERATING MODES

This application MUST have two fully functional control options.

## MODE A — ANDROID TO ANDROID

Phone A = Controller.

Phone B = Receiver.

Both phones install the same unified Mahi Remote Android application.

Phone A must be able to:

1. Generate or enter a receiver's six-digit pairing PIN.
2. Scan a receiver QR code.
3. Discover previously paired devices.
4. Request remote access.
5. Display the receiver's real-time screen.
6. Send taps and gestures.
7. Scroll, drag, and perform long presses.
8. Enter text into supported remote fields.
9. Perform supported Android system navigation.
10. Access authorized diagnostics and other existing remote features.
11. Disconnect the session immediately.

Phone B must be able to:

1. Generate a secure, expiring pairing code.
2. Display a QR code.
3. Receive incoming controller requests.
4. Show a proper device-owner approval screen.
5. Display controller identity and requested permissions.
6. Obtain MediaProjection consent.
7. Enable screen capture.
8. Establish the WebRTC session.
9. Receive remote input commands.
10. Display a persistent sharing notification.
11. Terminate sharing immediately.

The receiver must always retain control over authorization and session termination.

## MODE B — BROWSER TO ANDROID

A user opens the Mahi Remote web console using:

- Google Chrome.
- Microsoft Edge.
- Firefox.
- Brave.
- Other modern WebRTC-compatible browsers.

The browser becomes the controller.

The Android phone remains the receiver.

Required functionality:

1. Enter the six-digit receiver pairing PIN.
2. Scan or use supported QR pairing workflows where practical.
3. Send an actual pairing request through the backend.
4. Wait for receiver authorization.
5. Establish WebRTC negotiation.
6. Receive the actual Android video stream.
7. Show the complete receiver display.
8. Support browser mouse/touch interaction.
9. Support keyboard text input where possible.
10. Support Back, Home, Recents, and other supported system actions.
11. Support scrolling, swiping, long presses, and dragging.
12. Provide a genuine fullscreen viewing experience.
13. Show real connection status and diagnostics.
14. Support immediate emergency disconnection.

Browser-to-Android and Android-to-Android must share the same receiver implementation and session protocol.

Do not create two separate, incompatible Android screen-capture architectures.

